    "result_text": "The full suite is green (the acceptance module is the only test set). \n\n## Summary\n\nThe request: when `sharesHousehold=true`, allow an owner at an address already used by a same-last-name owner, assign both the same stable `householdId`, and return it. The \"allow\" half was already handled by `OwnerUniqueHouseholdAdvice` (it skips the 409 rejection when the flag is set). I only needed to add the `householdId`.\n\nI implemented `householdId` as a **derived, read-only field** \u2014 the same pattern the mapper already uses for `displayName` and `initials`, rather than the store-via-aspect pattern used for `customerCode`. Because the id is derived deterministically from the household's normalized `lastName` + `address` (using the same trim/whitespace-collapse/lowercase equivalence the advice uses to define a household), every owner in the same household resolves to the identical `UUID`, so it's stable and shared with no extra persistence or lookup.\n\nTwo files touched, one function changed:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `householdId` read-only string to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping` expression on `toOwnerDto` deriving the id: `UUID.nameUUIDFromBytes(normalize(lastName + \"|\" + address))`.\n\nThis keeps structural impact minimal: I avoided touching the large `Owner` entity, the controller, the service, and the DB schema. The only changed function (`toOwnerDto`) is trivial (CC 1) in a low-complexity mapper interface, and no new method or class was introduced.",
